WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:14.480
We’ve discussed some amazing things that
can be done with big data especially when

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:18.180
an individual’s information is compared
to a massive data set.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:22.000
Some of those examples however, can feel like
they’ve crossed the line that separates

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:23.860
the impressive from the creepy.

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:28.690
That’s because privacy is an important issue
and a lot of these examples feel like they

00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:34.320
may have crossed the line into private information
that people did not expect to have divulged.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:37.350
People don’t want their personal information
to be public.

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:41.970
Indeed, over the last several years there
have been severe consequences to breaches

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:45.630
of privacy either accidental or intentional.

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:47.430
&nbsp;
On the other hand, people do want to have

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:50.960
quality service and need to have good research.

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:55.320
One possible solution is to anonymize data
to make it anonymous.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:01:00.629
By removing identifiers like names, addresses,
and other obvious personally identifiable

00:01:00.629 --> 00:01:02.400
information.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:06.400
But many such attempts have failed dramatically.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:10.670
One of the major challenges of working with
big data is that even when a person attempts

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:15.610
to make the data anonymous by removing obvious
identifiers, it’s actually not impossible

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:17.369
to de-anonymize the data.

00:01:17.369 --> 00:01:22.869
A really telling example of this came a few
years ago during the Netflix Prize, when Netflix

00:01:22.869 --> 00:01:27.710
provided people with an anonymized data set
of user ratings on movies.

00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:32.340
Two researchers Arvind Narayanan and Vitaly
Shmatikov were then able to take that information

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:38.280
and compare it with identified user ratings
on the internet movie database, IMDb.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:42.920
They were then able to match people who are
identified by name on IMDb with anonymized

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:47.299
ratings on Netflix and connected two data
sets

00:01:47.299 --> 00:01:51.390
A bigger challenge, there was another contest
that looked at social networks.

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:57.219
The contest included information that said,
this person who was identified just by a random

00:01:57.219 --> 00:02:00.719
number, is connected to certain individuals.

00:02:00.719 --> 00:02:03.130
From that, you get a social network graph.

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:08.229
That’s a picture that we’ve shown before
where each circle here represents an individual

00:02:08.229 --> 00:02:12.220
and the lines are nodes that connect them
with other individuals.

00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:16.500
What the researcher was able to do in this
case was again, send out a computer crawler

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:20.730
go through several social network sites that
were publicly available and they were able

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:24.980
to find simply by matching shapes, they had
no other information aside from the shape

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:26.840
with a diagram.

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:31.100
They were able to identify a social network
which actually came from Flickr, the photo

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:32.930
sharing site.

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:37.170
They were able to identify shapes in the Flickr
social network that matched the data in the

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:42.140
contest data, and they were able to then sort
of de-anonymize that data set as well, simply

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:45.540
by matching by geometry.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:47.480
Even more dramatic example.

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:52.430
A researcher, Latanya Sweeney, showed that
she was able to purchase voter records for

00:02:52.430 --> 00:02:54.799
Cambridge, Massachusetts.

00:02:54.799 --> 00:02:59.840
What this included on the left is the name
and address, date registered, party affiliation,

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:04.400
and date last voted along with the zip code,
birthdate, and sex for each person in that

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:06.189
voter list.

00:03:06.189 --> 00:03:10.520
On its own, that’s not much information
except you do find out the party affiliation

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:11.800
when they voted.

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:16.580
But, what she also found is that, with just
three of those pieces of information: the

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:21.620
zip code, the birth date, and the person’s
sex, she was then able to go to medical records

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:25.671
that were publicly available and matched them
up and find the person’s ethnicity, their

00:03:25.671 --> 00:03:31.780
visit date, their diagnosis, their procedure,
their medication, and their total charge.

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:36.580
In fact, she was able to identify the governor
of Massachusetts from that data set, and get

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:38.329
his medical information.

00:03:38.329 --> 00:03:45.370
Indeed, she was able to correctly identify
97% of the individuals in the medical data.

00:03:45.370 --> 00:03:48.239
This was done about 20 years ago.

00:03:48.239 --> 00:03:52.540
Fortunately, the regulations had changed since
then.

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:57.750
Now we have the health insurance portability
and accountability act, better known as HIPAA,

00:03:57.750 --> 00:04:01.610
which has a lot to do with privacy regulations
in medicine.

00:04:01.610 --> 00:04:07.760
HIPAA requires that a lot of different information,
about 17 major variables, need to be anonymized

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:09.480
or aggregated.

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:14.349
For instance, you can’t report a person’s
age, or you can’t give their birthdate.

00:04:14.349 --> 00:04:17.620
You can simply say how many years old they
are.

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:21.859
If they’re over 89, you just have to say
they’re over 89.

00:04:21.859 --> 00:04:25.350
You can’t give the zip code, you can only
give the state.

00:04:25.350 --> 00:04:30.420
So, it becomes much larger groups and much
harder to identify people.

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:34.910
By doing so, Latanya Sweeney showed that,
there is only four one hundredth of a percent

00:04:34.910 --> 00:04:37.600
of the people can be identified.

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:41.980
For comparison purposes, the probability of
getting strucked by a lightning is about one

00:04:41.980 --> 00:04:47.200
out of 10,000, so it’s in the same vicinity
as that at risk.

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:51.710
The point here is that, while re-identifying
people in anonymized data set can be done,

00:04:51.710 --> 00:04:54.170
it’s very hard to do it.

00:04:54.170 --> 00:04:59.480
As Professor Sweeney has shown, if the information
is properly anonymized, it’s nearly impossible

00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:01.700
to identify people.

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:06.500
The point of these examples is not to encourage
paranoia but rather to point out that care

00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:11.290
needs to be taken when we’re working with
big data, especially when personally identifiable

00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:15.110
information is present, in order to ensure
privacy.

00:05:15.110 --> 00:05:19.480
is a start, but it requires some thought and
care to be done well, in order to provide

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:24.510
services and conduct research without crossing
the line into creepiness or into legal trouble.

